  But it also exposes a problem with the original framing worth naming before I ask anything else: [1min a single-user [0m
  [1mbrowser app, the only thing that changes a task is the user, five seconds ago.[0m "You marked 'buy milk' done" is not
  news. A notification system whose entire content is an echo of the user's own last click is ceremony, not a feature.
  The notifications that carry real information are the ones the [3mclock[0m generates, not the ones the user generates — a
  task became due, a task went overdue, a reminder came around. Those tell the user something they didn't already know.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what generates a notification. This decides whether tasks need date fields and a scheduler at all, and it's
  not cheap to flip later — time-based notifications need due dates on the task model and something polling the clock,
  so it shapes the data model from the first line.

  - [1mTime-based only (due soon / overdue)[0m — the notifications actually carry information. Requires a due-date field and a
    periodic check. Doesn't tell you anything about edits.
  - [1mChange-log only (task created / edited / completed)[0m — trivial to implement, needs no dates or clock, but every entry
    is something the user just did. Closer to an undo history than a notification feed.
  - [1mBoth[0m — most complete, roughly the sum of the work, and risks burying the useful time-based alerts under
    self-generated noise.

  I recommend time-based only. It's the version where "notify me about tasks I care about" means something a single user
  can't already see on screen.
